From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Wed, 22 Jun 94 12:34:23 -0700
Subject: [#94-6-95] digital cash and free banking 

> Would you mind telling us what these "more ferocious" regs are? I am
> unaware of any that apply specifically to backing digital cash.

If you back digital cash with FRNs, then you are running a bank. The
regulations you must conform to when you are running a bank are *much*
more stringent than the regulations you must conform to when you are
running a barter exchange or a commodity repository.

For starters, in all U. S. jurisdictions, you must get official
permission from the state to open a new bank. Such permission is very
difficult to get in most states. No such permission is required (in
most states) to open a new barter exchange or commodity depository.
So backing digital cash with anything of value other than fiat
currency would expose one to far less bureaucratic oversight.

Eric Watt Forste     (finger arkuat@netcom.com for the rest of my .signature)

